## Task 3 — Deepen the ruff rule set

**Plain-English problem.** The backend has no ruff configuration at all, so ruff
runs with its default rules only: basic pycodestyle errors and pyflakes. That
catches unused imports and syntax problems, but not the bug-shaped things — a
mutable default argument, a bare `except`, a loop variable captured by a closure.
The frontend by contrast has a real eslint config and strict TypeScript, so the
two halves of the codebase are held to very different standards.

**Change.** Add a `[tool.ruff.lint]` section to `backend/pyproject.toml`:

```toml
[tool.ruff.lint]
select = ["E", "F", "I", "B", "UP", "SIM"]
```

- `E`/`F` — what you already have.
- `I` — import sorting. Mechanical, auto-fixable.
- `B` — flake8-bugbear. This is the one that finds real bugs.
- `UP` — pyupgrade; the project is 3.12-only so this is safe.
- `SIM` — simplification hints.

**Expect a large number of findings on first run.** Work through them in this
order and **commit each step separately**:

1. `uv run ruff check --fix app ingest tender tests` — applies only the safe
   automatic fixes. Commit.
2. `uv run ruff check --fix --unsafe-fixes app ingest tender tests` — review the
   diff **file by file** before accepting. Commit separately.
3. Whatever remains, fix by hand.

If a rule proves genuinely noisy against this codebase's style, add a targeted
`ignore` entry with a one-line comment saying why — but do **not** silence `B`
wholesale, since it is the reason for doing this task.

**Verify.** After each step: `uv run ruff check app ingest tender tests` clean,
and `uv run pytest` at the baseline count. Lint changes must not change behaviour;
if the test count moves, you have broken something.

