## Task 4 — Run the backend test suite in parallel

**Plain-English problem.** There are roughly 2,100 backend tests and they run one
after another on a single core. Every local run and every CI run pays that. The
suite is the main feedback loop, so its speed sets the pace of everything else.

**Change.**

1. Add `pytest-xdist` to the `dev` dependency group in `backend/pyproject.toml`.
   Per the dependency policy in AGENTS.md, justify it in the commit message: it
   is a dev-only test runner, not a runtime dependency, and parallel process
   scheduling is not something to write by hand.
2. Update `addopts` in `[tool.pytest.ini_options]`:

   ```toml
   addopts = "-m 'not integration and not tender_eval' -n auto"
   ```

**Watch for this.** Some tests are order-dependent or share state (temp
directories, module-level caches, fixture-scoped DB objects) and will fail only
under parallel execution. That is a real finding, not an xdist problem — a test
that only passes in a fixed order is a test you cannot trust.

If tests fail under `-n auto`:

- Identify each failing test and **why** it is not isolated.
- Fix the isolation problem if it is small and obvious.
- If it is not, mark that specific test with `@pytest.mark.xdist_group(name="...")`
  or move it to a serial run, and **list every test you had to special-case in
  the commit message**. Do not paper over a large number of them — if more than
  about five tests need special-casing, stop and report instead.

**Verify.**

```bash
cd backend
time uv run pytest
```

Same pass/skip counts as baseline, in less wall-clock time. Record both the old
and new timings in the commit message.

