## Task 7 — Split `mcp_bridge/server.py` (optional; highest risk; do last)

**Plain-English problem.** `backend/app/mcp_bridge/server.py` is 4,270 lines
registering **69 MCP tools** — the entire surface the agent runtime can call. It
was touched in 26 of the last 143 commits. Same problem as Task 6, with a sharper
edge: this file is what Pi sees.

**Why this is the riskiest task.** Tools are registered by an `@mcp.tool`
decorator that runs **at import time**. If you move a tool into a new module and
that module is never imported, the decorator never runs, the tool silently
vanishes from the agent's toolset, and **the application still starts perfectly
normally**. There is no error. The agent just quietly loses an ability.

**Change.** Split into `backend/app/mcp_bridge/tools/` by domain — for example
`documents.py`, `cost_plan.py`, `procurement.py`, `tender.py`,
`platform_knowledge.py`, `workspace.py`, `profile.py`.

Use an explicit registration function per module rather than relying on import
side effects:

```python
def register(mcp) -> None:
    @mcp.tool
    async def ...
```

and call every `register()` explicitly from `server.py`. This makes a forgotten
module a visible missing call rather than an invisible missing import.

**The mandatory safety net — build this FIRST, before moving any tool.** Add a
test that asserts the exact set of registered tool names:

1. Enumerate the registered tool names from the `mcp` object.
2. Assert the set equals a checked-in expected list of all 69 names.
3. Commit this test **on its own, before any split work begins**, and confirm it
   passes against the current unsplit file.

This test is what converts "a tool silently disappeared" into "CI is red".
`backend/tests/mcp_bridge/test_mount.py` is the closest existing test and is a
reasonable place to look for how to reach the server object.

Then move **one domain group per commit**, running the tool-name test every time.

**Verify.** After every commit: the tool-name set test passes with all 69 names,
`uv run pytest tests/mcp_bridge/ -v` passes, and the full suite is at baseline.

**Stop and report if:** the tool count changes at any point, or you cannot
enumerate tool names from the `mcp` object cleanly — in that case the safety net
cannot be built, and **this task must not proceed without it**.

